    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The weirdness of entanglement comes from the fact that it seems to violate our classical intuition about space and time. Specifically, it's the non-locality aspect that trips people up.\n\nIn classical physics, if you measure the state of one particle, you can predict the state of another particle with certainty, based on the correlation between them. But with entanglement, measuring the state of one particle instantly affects the state of the other, regardless of the distance between them. This means that the information about the state of one particle is not transmitted through space, but rather is somehow \"entangled\" with the state of the other particle.\n\nThe weird part is that this effect happens even when the particles are separated by large distances, and even when they're not in direct causal contact. It's as if the information about the state of one particle is somehow \"linked\" to the state of the other particle, in a way that transcends space and time.\n\nOne way to think about it is to consider the EPR paradox, which was first proposed by Einstein, Podolsky, and Rosen in 1935. They showed that if entanglement were possible, it would imply that the state of one particle could be instantaneously affected by",
